course: dictionary
lesson: 9626
title: Stubby means short and thick
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can tell you a lot about the thing it describes. Stubby is a describing word, and it means short and thick, not long and thin. «At midday the Sun is highest and your shadow is short and stubby.» The clue is short: a stubby shadow is a short, thick one.
explain: Context clues help you check. «The Eurasian lynx has a short stubby tail.» Here short sits right next to stubby, so the two words tell you the same thing. Stubby does not mean long, sharp or soft.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. Swap stubby for short and thick: «a short, thick tail» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. You can also swap it for a word from the same family: stubbier means shorter and thicker, and stubbiest means the shortest and thickest.
fact: Stubby means short and thick.
fact: «Stubby fingers» are short, thick fingers, and «a stubby pencil» is one that is short and thick.
fact: Stubby is an adjective, so it describes a noun. Stubbier and stubbiest are its comparing forms.
fact: «Tardigrades», or water bears, are tiny animals less than a millimetre long, and they walk on eight stubby legs.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, stubby also has a grown-up meaning, so if you meet it in that sense, ask an adult or check a dictionary.
exercise: «The Eurasian lynx has a short stubby tail.» What does stubby mean here? | Very fast | *Short and thick | Soft and furry | Long and thin
exercise: «Scoop a clump of wet moss and you may meet a tardigrade, lumbering about on eight stubby legs.» Which animal has eight stubby legs? | Roe deer | *Tardigrade | Lynx | Hare
exercise: «At midday your shadow is short and stubby.» Which word means the same as stubby here? | *Short and thick | Long | Shiny | Soft
test: Ella has stubby fingers. What does that tell you about her fingers? | Cold and wet | *Short and thick | Long and thin | Very clean
test: Which word is the comparing form of stubby, meaning shorter and thicker? | stubbies | *stubbier | stubbing | stubbiest
exam: Which thing is most likely to be stubby? | *A short, thick candle | A long river | A giraffe's long neck | A thin hair
